## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical IAM policy construction tying S3 prefixes to service identities, a common beginner task for real-world isolation.

## Key Concepts
- IAM policies and bucket policies
- s3:prefix and path-based access control
- Least privilege and revocation testing
- Trust policy basics for role assumption

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:*",
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::logs-campus/*"]
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:PutObject", "s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::logs-campus",
        "arn:aws:s3:::logs-campus/logs/${serviceName}/*"
      ],
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {"s3:prefix": "logs/${serviceName}/"}
      }
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::logs-campus/logs/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you enforce encryption at rest for these objects?
- How would you audit access attempts and detect violations?
- How would you handle new services joining without updating policies frequently?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes capabilities in cross account replication, data governance, per tenant encryption, and auditability at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Cross account IAM roles and trust policies
- S3 replication configuration with destination bucket
- KMS key policies and encryption context per tenant
- TLS enforcement and bucket policies
- Audit via CloudTrail and DynamoDB/Lambda

## Code Example

```json
{
  "ReplicationConfiguration": {
    "Role": "arn:aws:iam::A:role/ReplicateToB",
    "Rules": [
      {
        "Status": "Enabled",
        "Prefix": "tenant-id-*/",
        "Destination": {
          "BucketAccountId": "AccountB",
          "Bucket": "arn:aws:s3:::dest-bucket",
          "StorageClass": "STANDARD"
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

